<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE COMP RESULT IS WHAT THE COLUMN SHOWS, AND THE CUTS ARE MARKED
  (plan 53 §4.4 / P7, QBX-157).

  TWO THINGS THIS CASE EXISTS FOR, and the first one is a debt A0b left
  written down in the code. `STakeStackRendererInline::draw` has drawn the
  COMP RESULT segment by segment since QBX-178 — before that a comped column
  drew the ACTIVE take across its whole width, so the picture showed material
  the column does not play and hid material it does (QBX-156's X10). That fix
  shipped with a note saying so: reverting the branch to the old active-take
  draw passed the WHOLE suite, because it is pure PAINT and nothing in the
  suite looked at the pixels. §4.4 allocated the pixel gate to this ticket.
  It is below, and it was measured to bite.

  The second is the CUT MARKS, which are new here: thin, low-contrast marks
  at every boundary of the comp walk and at the column's own two edges.

  THE SHAPE. One 4 s column over test_sawtooth.wav, at the default 30 px/s:

    take 0 — FULL extent [0, 192000), reading the source from 0, ACTIVE
    take 1 — PARTIAL extent [0, 48000), reading the source from 144000
    map    — `0:1 ; 144000:0`

  so `takeIndexAt` answers, quarter by quarter:

    [0 s, 1 s)  take 1, over its own material            -> take 1 -> MATERIAL
    [1 s, 3 s)  take 1 still, past the end of it -> -1   ->          NOTHING
    [3 s, 4 s)  take 0                                   -> take 0 -> MATERIAL

  THE TWO TAKES DISAGREE ABOUT LOUDNESS IN THE FIRST QUARTER, and that is the
  whole reason take 1 is slipped to source second 3. Per-source-second RMS of
  test_sawtooth.wav is 0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055, so over column
  second 0 take 1 is the LOUD end of the file and take 0 is the quiet end:

    correct : LOUD / nothing / LOUD      (0.4055, silence, 0.4055)
    broken  : quiet / nothing / LOUD     (0.0667, silence, 0.4055)

  THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS CASE DID NOT GATE THE PAINT AT ALL, and the sabotage
  pass is what said so. It had both takes reading the source from 0, so the
  comp result and the active take drew the SAME material and differed only in
  the hole — and the hole comes out of `fillBodyByMaterial`, which rides
  `collectEnvelope`, i.e. the COLLECT terminal that A0b already gated. Every
  gap metric in this verb is a collect measurement. `waveMeanPct` is the ONE
  field that rides the DRAW, so the two builds have to disagree about the
  drawn LEVEL or the paint is not gated by anything. Measured on the first
  draft: reverting the comp branch to the old active-take-only draw passed
  this case. With the slip it does not.

  TWO SEGMENTS AND THREE RUNS, which is the point of writing the map this way
  round: the boundary at 1 s is in NO segment. It is where take 1's EXTENT
  ends, and the map says nothing about it. So a mark loop written over
  `compMap().segments()` — the obvious shape, and the wrong one — marks the
  boundary at 3 s and misses the one at 1 s, leaving the only visible
  discontinuity in the picture unexplained. Walking the run boundaries instead
  gets both, because `takeIndexAt` folds the extents in. `cutMarkPcts` below
  is what holds that apart: measured, a segments-only loop reports three marks
  at `-1,75,98` instead of four.

  THE MIDDLE IS THE GATE, and the fixture is built this way round on purpose.
  A no-material run at the START or the END of a column would merely SHORTEN
  the measured span (`assert-take-lane` takes the span from the outermost
  material column), so a leading or trailing hole reports gapCols=0 and the
  broken paint would differ only in a span number. Put it in the MIDDLE and
  the whole clip is still spanned, the hole is inside it, and the two builds
  disagree about the shape of the picture rather than about its width:

    correct : material, HOLE at 25%..75%, material   -> gapRuns 1
    broken  : material all the way across            -> gapRuns 0

  ...AND -1 IS NOT A FALL-BACK (§3.1 step 5). Take 0 covers the middle two
  seconds perfectly well and must NOT be drawn there: the map selected a take
  that has nothing to say, and silence is the honest picture of that. A build
  that fell back would look entirely reasonable and would fail here.

  WHY A PIXEL VERB AT ALL. Everything a script can reach through
  `collectEnvelope` sits BELOW the paint — which is how proposal 39 M2 and
  proposal 41 M5 each shipped a green gate over a broken paint, and how the
  comp-result draw went a whole ticket ungated. `assert-take-lane`'s
  `takeRow="-1"` addresses the track's OWN composite lane, which is the
  surface §4.4 calls the compact column.

  AND THE PICTURE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE SOUND. The render at the end asserts
  the same three quarters as ENERGY, through the audio path (`twCompColumn`),
  against the same fixture: source second 0 (RMS 0.0667), then silence, then
  source second 3 (0.4055). Per-source-second RMS of test_sawtooth.wav is
  0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055, so "material / nothing / material" is
  not a shape two different readings of the map could both produce. §4.4's
  requirement is that picture = sound; this is that stated as two
  measurements of one fixture rather than as an argument about shared code.

  THE CUT MARKS. Four of them, and the count is EXACT:

    0%    the column's left edge
    25%   where take 1 runs out of material (an EXTENT edge, no segment)
    75%   where the map hands the position back to take 0 (a SEGMENT)
    100%  the column's right edge

  The two inner ones come from the SAME run walk the paint makes, and they are
  deliberately of the two different kinds — see the note on the map above. The
  two outer ones are the case the brief is really about: a `takes-split` pass
  leaves two columns abutting with the same body colour over
  continuous-looking material, and without a mark that cut is invisible.

  The percentages are of the clip's MATERIAL span, so a mark can legitimately
  report a small NEGATIVE percentage: the span begins at the outermost column
  that carries material, and a column's first pixels can round to silence. It
  does not happen in THIS fixture any more — take 1's slip puts the loud end of
  the file at the clip's left edge — but it did while both takes read the source
  from 0, and scanning marks only inside the span cost this gate the left-edge
  mark on its very first run while reporting a confident three of four. See
  `SMainWindow::describeTakeLane`.

  WHICH MEANS THIS FIXTURE NO LONGER GATES THAT FIX, and the sabotage pass
  says so: clamping the mark scan back inside the material span now PASSES
  here, because take 1's slip put material in the clip's very first column and
  all four marks sit inside the span. It was measured to bite before the slip
  (three marks instead of four), it is recorded in the describe at the site,
  and no fixture in the suite re-asserts it. Gating it would need a case whose
  clip BEGINS in silence, which is a property of the describe rather than of
  this feature — written down here rather than quietly left out.

  The marks are two short TICKS at the clip's top and bottom rather than one
  full-height line, for a reason worth not undoing: an opaque full-height mark
  would leave its own column with no body pixels in it, so the classifier
  would read the mark as a GAP — a cut mark manufacturing the discontinuity it
  describes — and `spanFirst`/`gapRuns` would move in every composite-lane
  case already in the suite. `maxCutMarkHeightPct` below is the assertion that
  holds that shut — and it is here because the first sabotage pass found that
  nothing else could see it: a full-height mark at the column's RIGHT edge only
  moves `spanLast` one column in, which takes the mark back out of the span and
  leaves every other number in the report unchanged. Measured: 30 % of the band
  for the tick pair, 96 % for a full-height line. `gapRuns="1"` says the
  obverse: four marks and still exactly one hole.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_comp_backdrop">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The partial take, SLIPPED to the loud end of the file. `activate="0"`
         keeps take 0 active, which is what makes the broken paint's answer
         (the active take, everywhere) different from the right one — and
         `startOffset` is what makes the difference VISIBLE rather than only
         structural. See the header. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="144000"
              index="-1" activate="0" at="0" length="48000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-192000;0-48000" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- The map: take 1 from the column's start — so it runs out of material
         at 1 s with no segment there — and back to take 0 at 3 s. -->
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="0" take="1"/>
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="144000" take="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="0:1;144000:0" activeTake="0"
                        takeAtPos="96000" expectTake="-1"/>

    <!-- THE GRID MUST BE OFF, and after every layout change — a
         non-emphasised grid line is drawn in EXACTLY the clip-body colour and
         OVER the lanes, so it reads as a full-height column of material and
         drags the span and every percentage off it. See take_lane_domain.qxa's
         header for the measurement. -->
    <grid-disable/>

    <assert-take-lane trackPath="0" takeRow="-1"
                      grabWidth="900" grabHeight="600"
                      grabPng="take_comp_backdrop_composite.png"
                      minGapCols="1" gapRuns="1"
                      firstGapStartPct="25" firstGapEndPct="75"
                      pctTolerance="3"
                      cutMarkRuns="4" cutMarkPcts="0,25,75,100"
                      minCutMarkCols="4"
                      minCutMarkHeightPct="20" maxCutMarkHeightPct="55"
                      minWaveMeanPct="40" maxWaveMeanPct="55"/>

    <!-- ...AND THE SOUND AGREES WITH IT, over the same three quarters. -->
    <render filename="tcb_comped.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- The comped first quarter is the LOUD source second, not the quiet one
         the active take would have put there. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tcb_comped.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.36" maxRms="0.45"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tcb_comped.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="96000"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.005"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tcb_comped.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.36" maxRms="0.45"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
